{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/path-cli/src/cmd_pathbase.rs","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-02T15:45:15.654504Z","id":"38e3a60dbecfecc0e9a3ef7d0003a13627fd77b51d3a31de6727f67b5ec82783","body":{"detail":"On any failure stream_and_clean_up sends delete_graph and discards its result (`let _ =`, line 897). The interrupt branch then tells the user \"the partial graph ... was deleted\" (lines 899-902), and the other branches return an error that does not mention the graph at all. Two things make that untrue in practice. First, the common reason to end up here is a transport failure after three retries, and the DELETE goes over the same connection, so it is likely to fail too; the graph then stays on the server, mutable and half-filled, and the user is not told its id. Second, on the server this client targets (empathic/pathbase#486), DELETE .../graphs/{id} removes the graph row and its membership rows but not the path rows or their steps, so every batch already sent stays in the repo as a path with no graph.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":894},"end":{"line":902},"content_hash":"f4a57b093a0146bccbc0c4430c1a94783bb601b5e6bc439af208e6ad33c803bb"},"suggested_fix":"Look at the DELETE result: say the graph was deleted only when it was, and otherwise name the graph id that remains. The path cleanup itself needs the server change (graph delete removing the member paths of a mutable graph); until that ships, do not claim the upload was removed.","summary":"Cleanup reports the partial graph as deleted without checking the DELETE, and the DELETE does not remove the uploaded paths","tags":["review","review:pr-305","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/path-cli/src/cmd_pathbase.rs","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-02T15:45:15.654605Z","id":"9e348643f4fb148fd4e633c59bb08f382beb2cde9d6cd4c784d140fee90e5cb9","body":{"detail":"tokio::signal::ctrl_c and tokio::signal::unix::signal replace the default signal behaviour for the rest of the process once a listener has been registered (https://docs.rs/tokio/latest/tokio/signal/unix/struct.Signal.html#caveats). watch() registers both and nothing undoes it, and the spawned task only runs while block_on is polling the shared current-thread runtime (lines 335-345). Consequences: (1) after the first Ctrl-C the cleanup DELETE at line 897 runs on the 120 s batch client with no race against a second signal, so a server that has stopped answering holds the terminal for up to two minutes and a second Ctrl-C does nothing; the same applies to the fallback graphs_post at line 909. (2) After a streamed upload returns, Ctrl-C and SIGTERM are swallowed for whatever the process does next. Today `path share` and `p export pathbase` make one upload and exit, but any caller that uploads several documents in one process, or keeps running after an upload, becomes unkillable except by SIGKILL once one upload was large enough to stream. (3) The retry backoff is a std::thread::sleep (line 826), so a signal during it is only seen when the next request starts.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":693},"end":{"line":711},"content_hash":"a9b678614105d879142e1c4b499e4acadc571ceab23de9d38ff0ed16d50dbcc4"},"suggested_fix":"Scope the listener to the upload: keep the JoinHandle and abort it when stream_and_clean_up returns, and after cleanup restore the default disposition or exit with the conventional 130/143 status. Race the cleanup DELETE against a second signal (or give it a short timeout), and sleep the backoff inside block_on with tokio::time::sleep raced against interrupt.wait().","summary":"Interrupt::watch installs SIGINT and SIGTERM handlers that stay for the life of the process, and nothing after the first signal is interruptible","tags":["review","review:pr-305","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/path-cli/src/cmd_pathbase.rs","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-02T15:45:15.654667Z","id":"8a74fd993651807a74332df67bc8a5c410a955c95d81c98627bf8a5cfc72d3e4","body":{"detail":"graphs_post_streamed creates the graph with paths: [] and then appends. On the server this targets (empathic/pathbase#486), an authenticated POST /graphs with no paths is created `mutable`, a document that carries its paths is created `frozen`, and no route freezes a graph. The client sends nothing after its last batch, so the outcome of `path share` depends on document size: at or under BATCH_BUDGET the shared graph is frozen and can no longer change; over it, the graph and its paths stay mutable indefinitely, and anyone with write access to the repo can keep changing what the share link shows. Callers (crates/path-cli/src/cmd_export.rs:967-988) print the same summary in both cases.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":855},"end":{"line":861},"content_hash":"74e77aef18f5c0022c6ea9dd78f07dcb9484ccc12102994d590da5d1f00b1a79"},"suggested_fix":"Finalize the upload once the server offers a way to: call the freeze operation after the last batch and treat its failure like a failed batch. Until then, note in the CHANGELOG entry and CLAUDE.md that streamed uploads end mutable.","summary":"A streamed upload leaves its graph mutable; the same document under 4 MiB is frozen","tags":["review","review:pr-305","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/path-cli/src/cmd_pathbase.rs","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-02T15:45:15.654720Z","id":"c949ac55b9db6408dba7af3f077e0826e7eec0886aaebc59a3f49169e26fa738","body":{"detail":"The fallback condition is a 404 or 405 on any path's first batch (opening_path is `bi == 0`, line 979). A server that has the route also answers 404 there when the graph or repo does not exist, and for the second or later path of a multi-path document the first path has already streamed successfully, which proves the routes exist. In both cases the client prints \"does not support streamed upload\" and re-sends the whole document in one request. A server without the route answers 405 (the path has a GET), as the comment in the PR description says.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":903},"end":{"line":910},"content_hash":"97d97cf22bef1590afb96159a9adcec5be6f8271a05a93dd2a7c83d3c3b4a81a"},"suggested_fix":"Fall back only on the first path's open request (pi == 0), and prefer 405 alone if no deployed server answers 404 for a missing POST on that path.","summary":"The no-batch-routes fallback also fires on a 404 that means the graph or repo is gone, and on later paths","tags":["review","review:pr-305","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/path-cli/src/cmd_pathbase.rs","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-02T15:45:15.654772Z","id":"71ec9e1c5d12ad14042d162a4358ad773e7f2b97947bee40ccfa490e9afec016","body":{"detail":"The function is given the writer's output as one string plus the step ids separately, and recognises lines by the byte prefixes `{\"Step\":` and `{\"Signature\":{\"target\":\"step:`. That ties batching to serde's key order for SignatureBody and to step_ids staying in lockstep with the Step lines. The lines exist as typed toolpath::v1::jsonl::JsonlLine values before they are written, where the kind, the step id, and the signature target are fields.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":572},"end":{"line":617},"content_hash":"9de6781aa971064359aed22c22ff811c5e0d24a7646b79fff643b2f45969c5e5"},"suggested_fix":"Build the batches from JsonlLine values (serialize each line once, keep its kind and step id beside the string) so the packer matches on the enum and takes one input.","summary":"pack_batches re-derives line kinds from serialized text and a parallel id list","tags":["review","review:pr-305","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/path-cli/src/cmd_pathbase.rs","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-02T15:49:14.756220Z","id":"cff0f1249e76eef90590272e3ba90b8ad1db2ef1e36b4b161904386e2ed4ef0c","body":{"detail":"Client (crates/path-cli/src/cmd_pathbase.rs): line 897 is `let _ = block_on(client.delete_graph(owner, repo, &graph_id));`, so a failed DELETE is indistinguishable from a successful one. Lines 899-902 then bail with \"the partial graph {} was deleted\" unconditionally. The other error branches (lines 911-916) name only {owner}/{repo} and the failure; none prints the graph id, and nothing earlier does either (graphs_post at 465-488 prints nothing; stream_paths at 968-975 prints only path/batch counters). A transport failure reaches this code after BATCH_RETRIES (line 820), and the DELETE is sent with the same `client` to the same base URL, so nothing makes it more likely to get through. CHANGELOG.md:23-24 repeats the claim (\"the partly uploaded graph is deleted\"). Server (empathic/pathbase#486 head 1462db4): crates/pathbase-api/src/graphs.rs:676 documents the 204 as \"Graph deleted; constituent paths remain in the workspace.\"; the handler (688-698) calls pathbase_db::graphs::delete, which is a bare `graph_entity::Entity::delete_by_id(id)` (crates/pathbase-db/src/graphs.rs:215-218); ARCHITECTURE.md:321 says the cascade removes junction rows only. So every batch already accepted stays as a path with no graph. Kind `concern` is right: a wrong status message and leftover data on a failure path, not a failure of the upload itself.","kind":"comment","references":"38e3a60dbecfecc0e9a3ef7d0003a13627fd77b51d3a31de6727f67b5ec82783","summary":"confirmed: the DELETE result is discarded, the interrupt message asserts deletion regardless, and the server's graph delete keeps the uploaded paths","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/path-cli/src/cmd_pathbase.rs","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-02T15:49:14.756255Z","id":"15bf77e9c3d0a1e5f76eb0c47e5990eddb431451d345a77139e79fbfcf3cd110","body":{"detail":"Tokio 1.52.3 (Cargo.lock) documents the caveat on both APIs used at lines 701 and 738: src/signal/ctrl_c.rs:20-31 and src/signal/unix.rs:334-344, \"an OS signal-handler is installed which replaces the default platform behavior ... for the duration of the entire process ... Even if this Signal instance is dropped, subsequent SIGINT deliveries will end up captured by Tokio, and the default platform behavior will NOT be reset\". Nothing in cmd_pathbase.rs keeps the JoinHandle from line 699 or restores a disposition, and the runtime is a `static OnceLock` current-thread runtime (335-345), so the spawned task is only polled inside block_on. (1) Line 897 is a plain `block_on(client.delete_graph(..))` with no select against the interrupt; `client` is built at line 863 with BATCH_TIMEOUT = 120 s (line 538), applied as the reqwest total timeout (line 363). After the first signal the listener task has finished, so a second Ctrl-C is captured by the installed handler and has no effect. (3) Line 826 is `std::thread::sleep(RETRY_BACKOFF * 2u32.pow(retries - 1))` (1 s, 2 s, 4 s); the listener task cannot run during it, so `interrupt.hit()` at line 807 is still false and the signal is only observed once the next block_on at 810 starts polling. (2) holds as stated and is hypothetical today: both callers go through run_pathbase_inner (crates/path-cli/src/cmd_export.rs:908-1017; cmd_share.rs:812, 857), which prints the summary and returns after one upload. Corrections that do not change the verdict: the fallback graphs_post at line 909 builds its own client with the 30 s default (lines 479, 353), so it is uninterruptible for up to 30 s rather than 120 s; \"unkillable except by SIGKILL\" overstates it, since only SIGINT and SIGTERM are captured (SIGQUIT and SIGHUP keep their defaults); and the handlers are installed when the spawned task is first polled, i.e. during the first batch's block_on, not inside watch() itself (tokio current_thread block_on returns as soon as the outer future is Ready, src/runtime/scheduler/current_thread/mod.rs:776-784). Kind `concern` is right: the user-visible effect today is a Ctrl-C that cannot be repeated while a hung server holds the cleanup request.","kind":"comment","references":"9e348643f4fb148fd4e633c59bb08f382beb2cde9d6cd4c784d140fee90e5cb9","summary":"confirmed: the handlers are process-lifetime, the cleanup DELETE and the backoff are not raced against a signal; three details in the text are slightly off","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/path-cli/src/cmd_pathbase.rs","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-02T15:49:14.756274Z","id":"5cc8cd152cc8632cc971cbc268b4a0cf85024b4e6c90752608aca58787f39c63","body":{"detail":"Client: graphs_post_streamed posts a shell with `paths: Vec::new()` (crates/path-cli/src/cmd_pathbase.rs:855-861), then stream_paths sends only open_graph_path / append_graph_path_steps and returns Ok(()) (981-1001); stream_and_clean_up returns `Ok(created)` with no further request (895). The generated client has no freeze operation: `grep -n freeze crates/pathbase-client/openapi.json` prints nothing, and the only mutability text there is the schema description at line 2705. The size switch is crates/path-cli/src/cmd_export.rs:967 (`body.len() <= BATCH_BUDGET || !streamable`), and both branches fall through to the same summary and URL output at 1006-1015. Server (empathic/pathbase#486 head 1462db4, crates/pathbase-api/src/graphs.rs): the authed create_graph passes `Mutability::Mutable` as `empty_mutability` (line 360); create_graph_inner uses it only when no path was sent with content and otherwise sets `Mutability::Frozen` (515-519; doc comment 451-455). pathbase_db::graph_mutations::freeze (crates/pathbase-db/src/graph_mutations.rs:685) has no caller outside tests: `grep -rIn 'freeze(' --include=*.rs .` lists only graph_mutations.rs itself and crates/pathbase-api/tests/it/{path_stream,graph_mutability}.rs, and the router (graphs.rs:41-85) has no freeze route. The batch routes gate on repo write permission only (reject_if_cant_write, graphs.rs:1261 and 1357), so any writer on the repo can keep appending to the shared graph. Neither CHANGELOG.md (lines 13-30) nor CLAUDE.md:227 says a streamed upload ends mutable. Kind `concern` is right: an undocumented difference in end state by document size, not a failing upload.","kind":"comment","references":"8a74fd993651807a74332df67bc8a5c410a955c95d81c98627bf8a5cfc72d3e4","summary":"confirmed: a streamed upload ends mutable with no way to freeze it, while a single-request upload of a document with paths is created frozen","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/path-cli/src/cmd_pathbase.rs","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-02T15:49:14.756286Z","id":"3261d592a7c4bd9837d904ad577c3c732d36dbe3839e42b85dba0aa43fc068a1","body":{"detail":"crates/path-cli/src/cmd_pathbase.rs:573-574 define `STEP = {\"Step\":` and `STEP_SIGNATURE = {\"Signature\":{\"target\":\"step:`; lines 584-592 match on them with starts_with and take the step id from `ids.next()`, a separate slice built at line 963 from `path.steps`. The signature prefix only matches because `target` is the first field of SignatureBody (crates/toolpath/src/jsonl.rs:133-136) and serde emits struct fields in declaration order; reordering those two fields, or a writer that ever emits a Step line not backed by `path.steps` in order, would silently split a step from its signatures or mislabel the Head line without a compile error. The lines are typed values immediately before serialization: Path::to_jsonl_writer constructs JsonlLine::PathOpen/ActorDef/Step/Signature/Head/PathClose at jsonl.rs:590, 603, 624, 627, 638, 649, 654 and hands each to write_line (666-674). One qualification for whoever picks this up: toolpath exposes no public function returning those JsonlLine values (the public surface is to_jsonl_writer / to_jsonl_string, jsonl.rs:587 and 659), so the suggested fix needs a small addition to the toolpath crate or the same line construction repeated in path-cli. Kind `suggestion` is right: nothing is wrong with the current output.","kind":"comment","references":"71ec9e1c5d12ad14042d162a4358ad773e7f2b97947bee40ccfa490e9afec016","summary":"confirmed: pack_batches classifies lines by serialized byte prefix and a parallel id slice; the typed lines exist in the writer but are not exposed today","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/path-cli/src/cmd_pathbase.rs","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-02T15:49:14.756299Z","id":"dced8b21ec355dd9ea6f19dd6b460f5d7913af816db53bf12567bfb84e016f49","body":{"detail":"crates/path-cli/src/cmd_pathbase.rs:903 falls back on `BatchFailure::Status(404 | 405, _)` when `e.opening_path`, and opening_path is `bi == 0` (line 979) inside `for (pi, path) in paths.iter().enumerate()` (949), with no condition on `pi`. So a 404 on the open request of path 2..n, after path 1 streamed successfully, prints \"does not support streamed upload\" (904-907) and re-sends the whole document through graphs_post (908-909). On a server that has the route (empathic/pathbase#486 head 1462db4, crates/pathbase-api/src/graphs.rs), open_graph_path returns ApiError::NotFound from resolve_repo (1260 -> 139-145), reject_if_cant_write (1261 -> 165-166) and resolve_graph_in_repo (1262 -> 185-188), i.e. when the owner, repo or graph is missing or not visible; the client test at cmd_pathbase.rs:2233-2247 covers only a 404 on a later batch of the same path, not on a later path's open. A server without the route answers 405: on pathbase origin/main `/api/v1/u/{owner}/repos/{repo}/graphs/{id}/paths` is registered with `get(list_graph_paths)` only, and the client test at 2139-2147 mocks exactly `405 Method Not Allowed`. CHANGELOG.md:28-30 and CLAUDE.md:227 describe the fallback as deleting \"the empty graph\", which is not the case on a later path. Kind `suggestion` is right: the graph was created by the same client moments earlier, so a 404 from a missing graph or repo needs a concurrent delete, and the result is a misleading note plus one whole-document retry.","kind":"comment","references":"c949ac55b9db6408dba7af3f077e0826e7eec0886aaebc59a3f49169e26fa738","summary":"confirmed: the fallback keys on the first batch of any path and on 404 as well as 405, so it also fires where the routes are known to exist","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/path-cli/src/cmd_pathbase.rs","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-02T15:54:31.343707Z","id":"cfeccd9d837924d189d7e1f1e46111f8d7ef3764da245b63d8f8c44c91065e77","body":{"detail":"The server in empathic/pathbase#486 already has DELETE .../graphs/{id}/paths/{path_id} (crates/pathbase-api/src/graphs.rs:1016-1025 there, graph_mutations::delete_path), which deletes a mutable path and its steps under the graph and path locks. stream_paths knows the server path_id of every path it opened (line 986), so cleanup can delete those paths and then the graph. That removes the stranded paths today; a batch still executing on the server when the graph DELETE arrives can only be closed on the server side.","kind":"comment","references":"38e3a60dbecfecc0e9a3ef7d0003a13627fd77b51d3a31de6727f67b5ec82783","summary":"Correction to the suggested fix: the client can remove the paths itself, without a server change","tags":["session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/path-cli/src/cmd_pathbase.rs","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-02T18:54:28.803922Z","id":"023dab98e98c3fb54509ff133ac7facaac46ef3944b1025bd9e4a03392c609fb","body":{"kind":"resolve","ref":"git:1a6c0f8","summary":"Fixed in 1a6c0f8: the streamed upload installs no signal handler. Interrupt, sigterm() and the select in post_batch are gone, so Ctrl-C and SIGTERM end the process the default way, including during the cleanup DELETE and the retry backoff. Evidence: `grep -c -i 'interrupt\\|sigterm\\|tokio::signal' crates/path-cli/src/cmd_pathbase.rs` prints 0 at 1a6c0f8; the PR's ci check passes on that commit (https://github.com/empathic/toolpath/actions/runs/37040110643).","supersedes":"9e348643f4fb148fd4e633c59bb08f382beb2cde9d6cd4c784d140fee90e5cb9","tags":["reason:fixed","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"crates/path-cli/src/cmd_pathbase.rs","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-02T18:54:35.815009Z","id":"d772efd925941d0b04a4481b84f543069bd89757e65f87c4df2eea864f38d260","body":{"detail":"1a6c0f8 removed the interrupt branch, and with it the only message that claimed the partial graph was deleted. graphs_post_streamed still sends the cleanup with `let _ = block_on(client.delete_graph(owner, repo, &graph_id))` and returns an error that does not name the graph, and the paths already opened are not deleted before the graph.","kind":"comment","references":"38e3a60dbecfecc0e9a3ef7d0003a13627fd77b51d3a31de6727f67b5ec82783","summary":"Status at 1a6c0f8: the 'was deleted' message is gone, the unchecked DELETE and the stranded paths remain","tags":["session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
